>
  <p>
    The technical reference is <a href={`${DOCS_URL}/security.md`}>docs/security.md</a> and
    <a href={`${DOCS_URL}/crypto/README.md`}>docs/crypto</a> in the repository. To report a vulnerability, see
    <a href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/SECURITY.md`}>SECURITY.md</a>. Kura Vault has not had an independent audit yet.
  </p>

  <h2 id="guarantee">The core guarantee</h2>
  <p>
    Photos, videos, file names, location data, album names and vault names are encrypted on your device. Every key is
    either random or derived from a passphrase that never leaves the device, and is stored only wrapped: under a
    passphrase, under another key, or sealed to a user’s public key. The API, the database and the storage bucket hold
    only ciphertext and wrapped keys.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Someone with full access to all three still cannot view anything without a user’s master passphrase.</strong>
  </p>

  <h2 id="secrets">Three secrets</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Secret</th><th scope="col">What it opens</th><th scope="col">Who sees it</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Password</th><td>The API: a session on the server</td><td>The server, once per sign-in, to check a bcrypt hash</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Master passphrase</th><td>Your account’s private keys</td><td>Nobody. It is checked on your device</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Vault passphrase</th><td>A vault you created, on top of the master passphrase</td><td>Nobody</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The apps refuse a master passphrase that equals your password, because the server sees passwords.</p>

  <h2 id="keys">How the keys fit together</h2>
  <p>
    Every vault, album and item has its own random key. Each key is wrapped by the one above it, and the top of the
    chain is opened only by a passphrase on your device.
  </p>
  <pre><code>master passphrase ─Argon2id─► master key ─► your private keys
                                              (X25519 for sealing, Ed25519 for signing)
vault passphrase  ─Argon2id─► passphrase key   (vaults you created)

vault key   random, one per vault
  ├─ your copy        sealed to your public key, under your passphrase key
  ├─ each recipient   sealed to their public key, signed by you
  └─ wraps ─► album key   random, one per album
  └─ wraps ─► media key   random, one per item
                 └─ encrypts ─► thumbnail, preview, original, metadata</code></pre>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Key derivation:</strong> Argon2id v1.3, 3 iterations, 64 MiB of memory, a 16-byte random salt.</li>
    <li><strong>Encryption:</strong> AES-256-GCM with a fresh random 12-byte IV for every encryption.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sealing to a person:</strong> an ephemeral X25519 key exchange, HKDF-SHA256, then AES-256-GCM.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Signatures:</strong> Ed25519.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Binding:</strong> every encryption authenticates a label naming what it is and the rows it belongs to,
      so the server cannot move a key or a file to another user, vault, album or item.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="trust">Whose public key is this?</h2>
  <p>
    The server hands out public keys, so in principle it could hand out its own. Two signed statements, checked on
    your device, stop that. The server owner’s client signs a <strong>certificate</strong> for every user’s keys, and
    your device only seals a key to someone whose certificate verifies. Every shared key also carries the
    <strong>sharer’s signature</strong>, so the server cannot plant a key it knows.
  </p>
  <p>
    A new user’s keys are bound to their invite link. The link carries a 32-byte secret in its <code>#fragment</code>,
    which browsers never send to any server, and the owner’s client checks the binding before it certifies anyone.
  </p>

  <h2 id="threats">Threats and mitigations</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Threat</th><th scope="col">Mitigation</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {threats.map(([threat, mitigation]) => <tr><th scope="row">{threat}</th><td>{mitigation}</td></tr>)}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="not-hidden">What is not hidden</h2>
  <p>
    The server needs some information to sort, paginate and authorise. Anyone who compromises it can see:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Usernames, each account’s role and access level, and public keys.</li>
    <li>
      How many vaults, albums and items exist, which items are in which, who created each vault, and who it is shared
      with.
    </li>
    <li>Per item: photo or video, capture time, dimensions, duration, file size, and who uploaded it.</li>
    <li>Access patterns: when an account signs in and what it fetches.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="limits">Limits, by design or not yet built</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No passphrase recovery.</strong> A forgotten master passphrase makes everything only that account could
      open permanently unreadable. The owner cannot reset it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Revoking access is a server cut-off, not re-encryption.</strong> Someone who kept a key and later
      obtains ciphertext from a breach could still decrypt it. Key rotation is not built yet.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Download links already issued stay valid for up to 60 seconds</strong> after access is removed.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Deleting cannot reach copies that already left</strong>, such as downloads, operator backups or
      versioned buckets.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Read versus write is enforced by the server.</strong> What stops a view-only user from uploading is the
      API, not the cryptography.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Whoever opens an invite link first becomes that user.</strong> Links work once, for 24 hours. Send them
      over a channel you trust.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The owner is trusted to say who the users are</strong>, not to read anything. A malicious owner could
      certify a key of their own under someone’s name.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Hidden vaults are hidden from people looking at your screen</strong>, not from the server operator.
    </li>
    <li><strong>A compromised device is out of scope.</strong> Malware on an unlocked device can read what you can.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>A web client is code served by its host.</strong> A compromised host could serve a modified client. The
      strongest setup is the web client served by your own server.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="hosted-client">The hosted web client</h2>
  <p>
    Greytimelabs hosts a web client at kura-app.greytimelabs.com that can connect to any Kura Vault server. It is
    convenient, and weaker than the client your own server serves, in three ways:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Refresh tokens are kept in browser storage</strong>, because a cookie cannot cross sites. A script
      injection bug could steal a session, which reaches encrypted blobs and metadata but no passphrase or key.
    </li>
    <li><strong>The host is trusted by every server’s users</strong>, because it serves the code that handles passphrases.</li>
    <li><strong>The content policy is wider</strong>: it allows connections to any HTTPS origin.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="android">The Android app</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Screenshots and screen recording are blocked, and the recent-apps card is blank.</li>
    <li>Nothing is in a device backup. Sign-in tokens are in the platform keystore.</li>
    <li>Time in the background counts toward the lock time: 1, 5 or 15 minutes.</li>
    <li>
      Fingerprint or face unlock is optional and off until you turn it on. It keeps the account’s master key,
      encrypted by a hardware key that only a biometric match releases, never the device PIN. Vault passphrases are
      always typed.
    </li>
    <li>
      The app never asks to read your photo library. It sees only what the system picker returns, and deletes its
      temporary plaintext copy once it is uploaded.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="deployment">How a server is hardened</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>One published port, on loopback by default. The database and bundled storage sit on internal networks.</li>
    <li>Every container has a read-only root filesystem, no Linux capabilities and <code>no-new-privileges</code>.</li>
    <li>Passwords are bcrypt hashes; refresh and invite tokens are stored only as SHA-256 hashes.</li>
    <li>Five failed logins lock an account for 15 minutes. Requests are rate limited.</li>
    <li>The web image sends a strict Content-Security-Policy that limits connections to the server and its bucket.</li>
    <li>The installer and upgrade script check every file against the release’s published checksums.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="verify">Check it yourself</h2>
  <p>
    The web client, the Android app and the import tool are three independent implementations of the same format.
    A shared file of <a href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/crypto-vectors.json`}>test vectors</a> holds fixed inputs and the
    exact bytes expected from them, and all three test suites check it. The access and tampering rules are exercised
    against a real server in the web client’s API tests. Everything is in the
    <a href={REPO_URL}>repository</a>, under the MIT licence.
  </p>
</Article>
